  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Liking",
     "texto": [
      "As in English, in French the person who likes something is the subject: **J'aime le café** (I like coffee).",
      "**J'adore** = I love. **Je n'aime pas** = I don't like. **Je déteste** = I hate.",
      "When the object is a noun expressing a general liking, you use **le, la, les**: **J'aime le poisson**, **Je déteste les œufs**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Partitives and indefinite plural",
     "texto": [
      "For an unspecified amount of an uncountable food, the partitives **du, de la, de l'** are used: **du** pain (masculine), **de la** viande (feminine), **de l'**eau (before a vowel).",
      "For several unspecified items, the plural article **des** is used: **des fruits**. In English you often leave it out («I eat fruit»).",
      "Compare: **J'aime le café** (coffee in general) and **Je prends du café** (I have coffee: an amount)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Articles in the negative",
     "texto": [
      "In a simple negation, partitives and the indefinite plural usually become **de**: **Je ne prends pas de café**, **Je ne mange pas de fruits**.",
      "In some contrasts and with **être**, the partitive is kept: **Ce n'est pas du café, c'est du thé**.",
      "With **aimer**, the definite article is kept: **Je n'aime pas le café**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
